---
import GuidePage from "../../../components/v3/GuidePage.astro";
const faq = [
  ["Can you bring in a production manager for just a few days?", "Yes. It is in fact the most common case: a production manager supporting the production team for the load-in, the show day and the strike. ESE offers this format on a day rate."],
  ["What is the difference between a production manager and a technical director?", "The technical director designs the set-up and makes the calls (technical choices, suppliers, budget, drawings). The production manager keeps the site running day to day: schedule, access, co-activity, crews and suppliers, from the first truck to the last."],
  ["Does the production manager handle safety?", "They organise the site so that the planned safety measures are upheld (access, circulation, co-activity), working with the safety officer and the organiser. Safety files and regulatory inspections are the responsibility of other parties."],
];
---
<GuidePage
  lang="en"
  title="Event production manager: role, tasks, when to bring one in · ESE"
  description="What a production manager does on an event (schedule, access, co-activity, crews, suppliers), how the role differs from the technical director, and how to bring one in as support for a few days."
  kicker="Guide · Production management"
  h1="The event<br>production manager"
  maj="2026-10-19"
  answer="The production manager keeps an event site running: they build the load-in and strike schedule, organise access and co-activity, coordinate the crews and suppliers, and act as the interface with the organiser, the venue, the artists and the authorities. You can bring one in for a few days of support or for an entire tour."
  faq={faq}
  cta="Find a production manager"
  ctaHref="/en/contact?type=regie-generale&format=renfort"
  related={[["The role of the technical director", "/en/guides/directeur-technique-evenementiel"], ["The stage manager", "/en/guides/stage-manager-evenementiel"], ["Our production management", "/en/metiers/regie-generale"]]}
>
  <h2>What a production manager does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Build and keep to the schedule for load-in, the show period and the strike.</li>
    <li>Organise access, deliveries and co-activity on site.</li>
    <li>Manage the crews and technical suppliers day to day.</li>
    <li>Act as the interface with the organiser, the venue, the artists and the authorities.</li>
    <li>Adapt decisions to the realities on the ground, in real time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When is one needed?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The load-in involves many crews in a short time.</li>
    <li>The venue imposes strict constraints: hours, access, neighbours, other operators.</li>
    <li>The production team needs support for a few days.</li>
    <li>A cultural venue, a site or a tour needs year-round production management.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Alone or with a team</h2>
  <p>On a small event, one production manager is enough. On a festival or in a stadium, they build a production team (area managers, assistants) sized to the format and the operational risk. They work closely with the technical director and the stage manager.</p>

  <h2>What ESE does</h2>
  <p>We handle production management on our own or alongside a team already in place, from day-rate support to running a site or a tour. References: the Pope's visit to the Stade de France, A$AP Rocky at the Accor Arena.</p>
</GuidePage>
